WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.158
This is the narrated briefing
for a companion guide in the

3
00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:07.837
Federal Modernization Workbook:
Azure Security Engineering.

4
00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:10.119
One thing first: the guide is a draft,

5
00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:14.051
awaiting the editor's review, so
recheck it after that review.

6
00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:19.321
The guide engineers the security of an
agency's Azure estate on top of the landing

7
00:00:19.321 --> 00:00:22.075
zone, as ten gated steps
an operator can run:

8
00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:26.245
from the security baseline and
an inventory of public exposure,

9
00:00:26.245 --> 00:00:30.415
through DDoS protection, the web
application firewall, ingress,

10
00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:35.450
Azure Firewall, Private Link and Key
Vault, to policy, operations and evidence.

11
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.511
Its commands and service behavior
come from Microsoft Learn,

12
00:00:39.511 --> 00:00:44.050
read on November eleventh, 2026, and
its control statements from NIST.

13
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.175
The program's own choices
are called design guidance.

14
00:00:47.175 --> 00:00:49.950
Nothing in it was run
against a live subscription.

15
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.820
We'll go in four parts:
why, what, who, and how.

16
00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.620
Here's the idea the guide starts from.

17
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.774
A landing zone decides what
can be deployed, and where:

18
00:01:02.774 --> 00:01:06.501
the management group hierarchy,
the boundary policies and the

19
00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:10.157
exemption rule that Cloud Boundary
and Landing Zone designs.

20
00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:12.836
It doesn't decide what
the internet can reach,

21
00:01:12.836 --> 00:01:15.370
what leaves the estate,
or who can read a key.

22
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.883
Those are engineered one service at a
time, and each one has to be proved.

23
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:21.671
So the guide's goal is this.

24
00:01:21.671 --> 00:01:25.364
Every internet-facing service is
in an inventory with an owner,

25
00:01:25.364 --> 00:01:29.769
behind DDoS protection and a web
application firewall in prevention mode,

26
00:01:29.769 --> 00:01:31.758
and nothing can reach around them.

27
00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:35.353
Workloads leave Azure only
through an inspecting firewall

28
00:01:35.353 --> 00:01:39.020
and reach platform services
only through private endpoints.

29
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.332
Keys sit in vaults that only managed identities
and eligible administrators can use.

30
00:01:44.332 --> 00:01:46.870
And Azure Policy holds it all in place.

31
00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:51.325
A finding is ready for closure when
every applicable state has evidence;

32
00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:54.580
the authorizing official
or assessor decides.

33
00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.280
The workbook's books
already design most of this.

34
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.466
Cloud Boundary and Landing
Zone builds the hierarchy,

35
00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:08.218
the boundary policies, the
provisioning loop and the ownership.

36
00:02:08.218 --> 00:02:12.280
Security Operations and Telemetry
chooses the posture tool.

37
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.157
Network and Transport
owns the agency edge,

38
00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:20.142
and DNS, DHCP and IPAM
owns name resolution.

39
00:02:20.142 --> 00:02:25.318
What none of them gives is the service-by-service
engineering of the Azure controls

40
00:02:25.318 --> 00:02:29.368
a Zero Trust assessment tests: DDoS
protection on every public I P,

41
00:02:29.368 --> 00:02:34.243
a web application firewall in prevention
mode, origins only the edge can reach,

42
00:02:34.243 --> 00:02:39.268
egress through an inspecting firewall,
platform services reached only through

43
00:02:39.268 --> 00:02:43.993
private endpoints, and keys behind
role-based access with purge protection.

44
00:02:43.993 --> 00:02:48.595
Microsoft Learn's Azure network
security Zero Trust recommendations

45
00:02:48.595 --> 00:02:52.460
list those checks, and the
guide takes each one as a step.

46
00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:01.260
Here's the path on one page, each step
with a done-when and a way to validate it.

47
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.059
One: protect the hierarchy,
assign the security initiative,

48
00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.997
and get platform logs flowing.

49
00:03:06.997 --> 00:03:10.337
Two: put every public I P,
Front Door endpoint and

50
00:03:10.337 --> 00:03:13.761
application gateway in a
register with an owner.

51
00:03:13.761 --> 00:03:17.117
Three: DDoS protection
on every public I P,

52
00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:20.572
logged and alerting,
with one exercise run.

53
00:03:20.572 --> 00:03:24.011
Four: the web application
firewall in prevention mode.

54
00:03:24.011 --> 00:03:27.274
Five: origins that only
the edge can reach,

55
00:03:27.274 --> 00:03:30.922
T L S one point two at
least, and failover tested.

56
00:03:30.922 --> 00:03:33.418
Six: default routes to Azure Firewall,

57
00:03:33.418 --> 00:03:37.361
with threat intelligence and
intrusion detection in deny.

58
00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:40.486
Seven: platform services
with public access off,

59
00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.222
resolving through central private zones.

60
00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:46.910
Eight: vaults on role-based
access with purge protection.

61
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.885
Nine: policy that holds it,
with exemptions that expire.

62
00:03:50.885 --> 00:03:54.035
Ten: owners, cadence and evidence.

63
00:03:54.035 --> 00:03:59.530
Steps one and two come first; three to
eight can run in parallel by workload.

64
00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.180
Which cloud?

65
00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.430
Microsoft three sixty-five G C C pairs
with commercial Azure, so a G C C agency's

66
00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:14.680
subscriptions are usually commercial,
and every service here is available.

67
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.272
G C C High and D O D pair with Azure
Government, and Microsoft Learn's page,

68
00:04:19.272 --> 00:04:22.122
Compare Azure Government and global Azure,

69
00:04:22.122 --> 00:04:27.030
says a service there has all its features
unless the page lists a variation.

70
00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.080
A few matter.

71
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.884
Front Door Standard and Premium are
generally available in two U S Gov regions,

72
00:04:32.884 --> 00:04:36.580
and Private Link origins outside
Virginia are unconfirmed.

73
00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.021
On Application Gateway, Learn's web application
firewall F A Q says sovereign clouds

74
00:04:42.021 --> 00:04:46.297
have no bot protection rule sets
and no geo-filtering custom rules,

75
00:04:46.297 --> 00:04:51.505
so that Zero Trust test is recorded as
skipped, not applicable, rather than failed.

76
00:04:51.505 --> 00:04:57.042
Private DNS zone names differ, and
Lighthouse has no pim for delegated access.

77
00:04:57.042 --> 00:05:01.270
Whatever the cloud, sign in to
the one the subscription lives in.

78
00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:09.125
An agency's H R platform may run
on Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

79
00:05:09.125 --> 00:05:12.520
so the guide lines up the
equivalents on four clouds.

80
00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.094
For network-layer DDoS, Azure's
infrastructure protection is free,

81
00:05:17.094 --> 00:05:21.501
with Network or I P Protection on
top; A W S has Shield Standard and

82
00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:26.407
Shield Advanced; Google Cloud Armor
is always on behind load balancers.

83
00:05:26.407 --> 00:05:32.722
For the web application firewall: Azure
W A F, A W S W A F, Cloud Armor policies,

84
00:05:32.722 --> 00:05:37.983
and the O C I web application
firewall, regional or at the edge.

85
00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:42.056
For inspection, Azure Firewall
Premium and the O C I Network

86
00:05:42.056 --> 00:05:46.295
Firewall both offer intrusion
detection and T L S inspection.

87
00:05:46.295 --> 00:05:50.013
For private access to platform
services: Private Link,

88
00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:54.145
Google's Private Service Connect,
and O C I private endpoints.

89
00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:59.709
In the government clouds, A W S W A F
in GovCloud offers only A W S managed

90
00:05:59.709 --> 00:06:05.730
rule groups, and Oracle's government
availability isn't yet specified.

91
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.318
Now, who.

92
00:06:10.318 --> 00:06:14.199
Every role this work needs is
eligible through pim, not standing,

93
00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:16.544
as the Privileged Access guide sets up:

94
00:06:16.544 --> 00:06:19.474
Network Contributor for
the connectivity work,

95
00:06:19.474 --> 00:06:23.869
Resource Policy Contributor for
Azure Policy, Key Vault Administrator

96
00:06:23.869 --> 00:06:27.605
for vault settings, and Security
Admin for Defender for Cloud.

97
00:06:27.605 --> 00:06:31.085
The guide's RACI follows Cloud
Boundary and Landing Zone's

98
00:06:31.085 --> 00:06:34.493
chapter on who owns the boundary,
and it's design guidance.

99
00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:37.882
The policy owner is accountable
for the decision records,

100
00:06:37.882 --> 00:06:40.142
the baseline and the evidence package.

101
00:06:40.142 --> 00:06:44.146
The platform owner is accountable
for the register, the DDoS plan,

102
00:06:44.146 --> 00:06:47.408
the web application firewall
and firewall policies,

103
00:06:47.408 --> 00:06:50.967
and the private DNS zones, with
the operator doing the work.

104
00:06:50.967 --> 00:06:55.005
The security monitor owns alert
response and DDoS incidents.

105
00:06:55.005 --> 00:06:57.799
Application owners answer
for their origins,

106
00:06:57.799 --> 00:07:02.270
certificates and vault contents,
and the assessor decides closure.

107
00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.770
Now how, starting with steps one and two.

108
00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:12.795
Step one adds the security
settings the landing zone needs.

109
00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:17.683
Protect the hierarchy, as Learn's page,
Protect your resource hierarchy, shows:

110
00:07:17.683 --> 00:07:21.043
require authorization to
create management groups

111
00:07:21.043 --> 00:07:23.945
and send new subscriptions
to a default group.

112
00:07:23.945 --> 00:07:28.851
Assign the Microsoft cloud security
benchmark and the fed-ramp initiative at the

113
00:07:28.851 --> 00:07:33.108
intermediate root in do-not-enforce
mode, which, per Learn's Azure Policy

114
00:07:33.108 --> 00:07:38.158
assignment structure, evaluates compliance
without enforcing; step nine enforces.

115
00:07:38.158 --> 00:07:41.506
Send every platform resource's
logs to the workspace,

116
00:07:41.506 --> 00:07:43.408
and turn on Defender for Cloud.

117
00:07:43.408 --> 00:07:47.513
Step two builds what nothing else
can be proved complete without:

118
00:07:47.513 --> 00:07:48.958
a list of what's exposed.

119
00:07:48.958 --> 00:07:52.136
Learn recommends an
inventory of every public I P

120
00:07:52.136 --> 00:07:55.470
and its protection status,
kept with Resource Graph.

121
00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:59.326
The register adds the owner, the
D N S names and certificates,

122
00:07:59.326 --> 00:08:02.945
and whether a web application
firewall covers each one.

123
00:08:02.945 --> 00:08:07.010
It's a map of the attack surface,
so keep it inside the boundary.

124
00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.085
Step three is DDoS protection.

125
00:08:13.085 --> 00:08:17.635
Every public I P in Azure gets
infrastructure protection at no cost.

126
00:08:17.635 --> 00:08:22.719
Learn's tier comparison says Network
Protection and I P Protection add adaptive

127
00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:26.817
tuning, attack analytics, metrics,
alerts and mitigation reports;

128
00:08:26.817 --> 00:08:31.522
Network Protection also adds the Rapid
Response team and cost protection.

129
00:08:31.522 --> 00:08:35.497
Choose Network Protection when
many public I Ps need coverage,

130
00:08:35.497 --> 00:08:39.472
and I P Protection for a few critical
ones, and record the choice.

131
00:08:39.472 --> 00:08:43.621
Both work at layers three and
four, so layer seven needs the web

132
00:08:43.621 --> 00:08:48.259
application firewall, and neither
covers a public I P on a NAT gateway.

133
00:08:48.259 --> 00:08:52.891
Create one plan, link each virtual
network, turn on diagnostic logs,

134
00:08:52.891 --> 00:08:57.522
and alert on the metric that reads
one while an attack is mitigated.

135
00:08:57.522 --> 00:08:59.797
Then hold it with policy in step nine.

136
00:08:59.797 --> 00:09:05.624
Exercise the response: Learn allows simulations
only through approved partners, only

137
00:09:05.624 --> 00:09:11.250
against your own protected I Ps, and one
partner, Red Button, is public cloud only.

138
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.620
Step four is the web application firewall,

139
00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.287
on Front Door globally or
Application Gateway regionally.

140
00:09:21.287 --> 00:09:26.101
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations for
Application Gateway say it plainly:

141
00:09:26.101 --> 00:09:30.838
detection mode logs an attack and
forwards it; only prevention mode blocks.

142
00:09:30.838 --> 00:09:34.617
The checks cover prevention
mode, request body inspection,

143
00:09:34.617 --> 00:09:39.399
the default rule set, bot protection,
rate limiting and diagnostic logging.

144
00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:42.437
Learn recommends the
latest default rule set,

145
00:09:42.437 --> 00:09:46.512
two point two when read, validated
in log mode before blocking.

146
00:09:46.512 --> 00:09:50.976
So create the policy in detection
mode with request body inspection on,

147
00:09:50.976 --> 00:09:53.624
naming the rule set version you validated.

148
00:09:53.624 --> 00:09:56.927
Add bot protection, which
is commercial Azure only,

149
00:09:56.927 --> 00:09:59.050
and a rate limit on the login path.

150
00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.517
Attach the policy, send the firewall
log to the workspace, and tune for at

151
00:10:03.517 --> 00:10:08.212
least a full business cycle, scoping
every exception to the narrowest rule.

152
00:10:08.212 --> 00:10:09.826
Then switch to prevention.

153
00:10:09.826 --> 00:10:12.162
Front Door's managed rules need Premium.

154
00:10:12.162 --> 00:10:15.370
In Azure Government, leave
out the bot rule set.

155
00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.320
Step five finishes the ingress.

156
00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:26.567
Front Door is global, at the Microsoft
edge with built-in network-layer DDoS

157
00:10:26.567 --> 00:10:31.645
protection; Application Gateway is
regional, in the agency's own network.

158
00:10:31.645 --> 00:10:34.207
Either works with the
firewall from step four.

159
00:10:34.207 --> 00:10:38.101
What matters is that nothing can
reach the origin around the edge,

160
00:10:38.101 --> 00:10:41.707
or an attacker skips both the
firewall and the DDoS controls.

161
00:10:41.707 --> 00:10:46.415
Use Private Link where the origin
supports it: Front Door Premium reaches

162
00:10:46.415 --> 00:10:51.045
App Service, Blob Storage, internal
load balancers and others privately.

163
00:10:51.045 --> 00:10:55.413
Where it doesn't, Learn's page, Secure
traffic to Azure Front Door origins,

164
00:10:55.413 --> 00:10:59.337
says to use the service tag and
the Front Door I D header together,

165
00:10:59.337 --> 00:11:03.483
because the tag's addresses are
shared by every Front Door customer.

166
00:11:03.483 --> 00:11:07.146
Set T L S one point two
or one point three only.

167
00:11:07.146 --> 00:11:10.923
Build health probes and two
origins in different regions,

168
00:11:10.923 --> 00:11:12.970
then stop one to prove failover.

169
00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:18.430
And migrate any classic Front Door
before March thirty-first, 2027.

170
00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:24.280
Step six inspects what leaves.

171
00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:28.296
Network and Transport owns the
agency's edge; inside Azure,

172
00:11:28.296 --> 00:11:33.317
Azure Firewall is the inspection point
for egress and east-west traffic.

173
00:11:33.317 --> 00:11:38.458
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations for
Azure Firewall check that outbound traffic

174
00:11:38.458 --> 00:11:43.084
routes through it, that threat intelligence
and intrusion detection are in

175
00:11:43.084 --> 00:11:47.343
alert and deny mode, that outbound T L
S is inspected, and that logs are on.

176
00:11:47.343 --> 00:11:50.840
Threat intelligence deny
needs Standard or Premium;

177
00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:54.417
intrusion detection and T L
S inspection need Premium.

178
00:11:54.417 --> 00:11:56.897
Create a Premium policy in alert mode,

179
00:11:56.897 --> 00:12:02.256
add a default route from each workload
subnet to the firewall, and log everything.

180
00:12:02.256 --> 00:12:06.391
After two weeks of reading the
alerts, move to deny through a draft,

181
00:12:06.391 --> 00:12:09.455
so every change lands at
once and can be reviewed.

182
00:12:09.455 --> 00:12:13.735
Learn warns that deploying a draft
replaces the whole applied policy,

183
00:12:13.735 --> 00:12:16.317
so live edits made after
the draft are lost.

184
00:12:16.317 --> 00:12:20.476
T L S inspection needs a trusted
certificate authority in Key Vault,

185
00:12:20.476 --> 00:12:22.770
and spokes talk through the hub.

186
00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.432
Step seven gives platform services a
private address in the agency's network.

187
00:12:31.432 --> 00:12:35.858
A private endpoint does that; D N
S decides whether clients use it.

188
00:12:35.858 --> 00:12:41.235
Learn's Key Vault Private Link page is
clear that name resolution is where private

189
00:12:41.235 --> 00:12:45.664
endpoints fail: the zone must exist,
be linked to the virtual network

190
00:12:45.664 --> 00:12:49.619
and hold the record, and on-premises
D N S must forward to Azure.

191
00:12:49.619 --> 00:12:52.783
So create each private
link zone once, centrally,

192
00:12:52.783 --> 00:12:56.420
in the connectivity subscription,
and link it to the hub.

193
00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.011
Create the private endpoint
with a zone group,

194
00:12:59.011 --> 00:13:02.420
which registers the record and
removes it with the endpoint.

195
00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:06.320
At scale, the Cloud Adoption
Framework's pattern lets policy do it:

196
00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:10.726
deny private link zones and public
endpoints in workload subscriptions,

197
00:13:10.726 --> 00:13:13.182
and deploy the zone group automatically.

198
00:13:13.182 --> 00:13:17.732
Then turn public access off, and add
conditional forwarders on premises.

199
00:13:17.732 --> 00:13:22.830
In Azure Government the zone names
differ, so policy needs those names.

200
00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:30.180
Step eight covers the vault
that managed identities read.

201
00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:33.777
The identities themselves are
in the Service Accounts guide:

202
00:13:33.777 --> 00:13:37.796
a workload that moves to Azure gets
a user-assigned managed identity,

203
00:13:37.796 --> 00:13:41.393
and continuous delivery uses
workload identity federation.

204
00:13:41.393 --> 00:13:46.857
Learn's page, Secure your Azure Key Vault,
recommends one vault per application,

205
00:13:46.857 --> 00:13:51.732
region and environment; role-based
access, not legacy access policies;

206
00:13:51.732 --> 00:13:56.775
pim for administrators; soft delete
and purge protection; autorotation;

207
00:13:56.775 --> 00:14:01.230
audit logging and alerts; private
endpoints; and tested backups.

208
00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:05.279
So create each vault on role-based
access with purge protection,

209
00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:06.730
and close its firewall.

210
00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:11.455
Give the workload's identity a data-plane
role at the vault, not the subscription.

211
00:14:11.455 --> 00:14:16.343
Rotate keys by a policy file, and
send audit events to the workspace.

212
00:14:16.343 --> 00:14:21.256
If a key is suspected compromised,
rotate and migrate its dependents first;

213
00:14:21.256 --> 00:14:24.140
disabling it first takes them offline.

214
00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:31.289
Step nine turns steps
three to eight into policy,

215
00:14:31.289 --> 00:14:36.302
because drift is the threat, as
Governance, Drift and Evidence explains.

216
00:14:36.302 --> 00:14:41.333
Gather the security definitions into
one initiative at the intermediate root:

217
00:14:41.333 --> 00:14:44.457
DDoS protection on virtual
networks and its logs,

218
00:14:44.457 --> 00:14:48.496
the web application firewall
enabled and in the specified mode,

219
00:14:48.496 --> 00:14:52.916
the private D N S and public-access
policies, and the Key Vault policies.

220
00:14:52.916 --> 00:14:55.952
Look up each definition's
name in the cloud you're in,

221
00:14:55.952 --> 00:14:57.977
because built-ins differ by cloud.

222
00:14:57.977 --> 00:14:59.949
Assign it in do-not-enforce mode,

223
00:14:59.949 --> 00:15:03.589
review compliance with the
owners, then switch to default.

224
00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:06.753
Run a remediation task
for what already exists.

225
00:15:06.753 --> 00:15:09.803
Every exemption gets a
category and an expiry.

226
00:15:09.803 --> 00:15:14.722
Learn's Azure Policy exemption structure
says mitigated means the intent is met

227
00:15:14.722 --> 00:15:18.541
another way, waiver means
non-compliance is accepted for a time,

228
00:15:18.541 --> 00:15:22.727
and an expired exemption is kept for
the record but no longer honored.

229
00:15:22.727 --> 00:15:25.280
Export compliance every month.

230
00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:33.330
Step ten puts every owner,
cadence and test in one place.

231
00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:38.682
The cadence is design guidance: weekly,
rerun the exposure register; monthly,

232
00:15:38.682 --> 00:15:44.118
the policy compliance export, exemptions
due, and false positives; quarterly,

233
00:15:44.118 --> 00:15:49.470
DDoS coverage and cost, firewall rules,
vault access and the rule-set version;

234
00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:53.207
twice a year, a Front Door
failover and a vault restore;

235
00:15:53.207 --> 00:15:57.030
and yearly, a DDoS simulation
with an approved partner.

236
00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.618
In a DDoS attack, the alert
pages the security monitor,

237
00:16:00.618 --> 00:16:05.592
and under Network Protection a severity
A request goes to Rapid Response.

238
00:16:05.592 --> 00:16:09.754
The metrics measure protected
coverage, not whether a setting exists.

239
00:16:09.754 --> 00:16:14.517
And each step supports controls in
NIST special publication eight hundred

240
00:16:14.517 --> 00:16:18.448
fifty-three, such as denial-of-service
and boundary protection,

241
00:16:18.448 --> 00:16:22.605
with the fed-ramp twenty X key
security indicators as a vocabulary.

242
00:16:22.605 --> 00:16:26.590
The steps support those
controls; the assessor decides.

243
00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:32.203
To close, the dangers.

244
00:16:32.203 --> 00:16:35.199
A web application firewall
left in detection mode,

245
00:16:35.199 --> 00:16:37.553
logging attacks that reach the backend.

246
00:16:37.553 --> 00:16:39.912
An origin reachable around the edge,

247
00:16:39.912 --> 00:16:44.316
or protected only by a service tag
every Front Door customer shares.

248
00:16:44.316 --> 00:16:46.315
A public I P nobody knows about.

249
00:16:46.315 --> 00:16:49.503
A firewall draft deployed
over newer changes.

250
00:16:49.503 --> 00:16:52.440
Commercial features assumed
in Azure Government.

251
00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:57.227
And disabling a key to contain a compromise,
which takes its dependents offline.

252
00:16:57.227 --> 00:16:59.065
Each has its guard in the steps.

253
00:16:59.065 --> 00:17:01.853
The guide is plain about
what it couldn't verify:

254
00:17:01.853 --> 00:17:06.427
Front Door and its Private Link origins
region by region in Azure Government,

255
00:17:06.427 --> 00:17:09.357
Front Door's firewall
limits in sovereign clouds,

256
00:17:09.357 --> 00:17:13.503
and which DDoS tiers and simulation
partners serve Azure Government.

257
00:17:13.503 --> 00:17:15.602
Nothing was run in a subscription.

258
00:17:15.602 --> 00:17:19.140
Remember that the guide is a draft
awaiting the editor's review.

259
00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:23.090
Its developer workbook with labs
and its training path are planned.

260
00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:23.910
Thank you.
